raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Do the links between substance use and friendship reported in racially heterogeneous schools also hold in American Indian (AI)–majority reservation schools? The authors expect weaker associations with nominating friends, being nominated, and choosing peers with similar use patterns.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This paper takes an\ninitial step toward understanding these dynamics by investigating the role of substance use in the formation of friendship ties within AI‑majority schools.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Consequently, we expect that substance use will show weaker sender,\nreceiver, and homophily effects in AI-majority schools than observed in heterogeneous populations.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How do reciprocity, triadic closure, and the distribution of nominations organize these networks? The authors expect strong reciprocity and closure but leave open whether the degree patterns indicate preferential attachment or constraints on popularity.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This cultural foundation translates the structural tendency for\nreciprocity into a normative expectation within social conduct, suggesting that reciprocal friendships may be especially\npronounced in AI adolescent networks [4].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we expect this tendency toward closure to be at least as strong as—and plausibly\nstronger than—that documented in less cohesive school settings, given the dense overlap of familial, school, and communal social spheres.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "We therefore explore the nature of degree effects, whether they indicate preferential\nattachment or egalitarian constraints, within these specific AI adolescent contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is depression associated with receiving fewer nominations, withdrawing from friendships, or choosing peers with similar symptoms in AI-majority schools?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\ntherefore examine which of these mechanisms—receiver effects, withdrawal-related sender effects, or homophily—\noperate in AI-majority school contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the strength of AI identity affirmation matter for sending nominations, receiving them, or choosing similarly identified friends? The authors explore these possibilities without a directional hypothesis.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Identity affirmation—positive feelings of belonging, pride, and commitment to one’s AI group—has been linked to\nresilience and psychological well-being in Indigenous youth [37], but its role in friendship network formation has not been\nexamined.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Given the exploratory nature of this investigation, we\ndo not formulate directional hypotheses but examine sender, receiver, and homophily effects empirically.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do the familiar patterns of same-gender and same-grade friendship recur in these schools, and does shared sexual orientation also organize friendship ties?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Substantively, gender and grade homophily are among the most robust findings in adolescent network research [9, 12]; we expect\nthese patterns to replicate in AI-majority schools, where gender- and grade-segregated activities similarly organize peer\ninteraction.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily has received limited attention in the literature, but documented social stratification along sexual identity lines in adolescent populations suggests\nthat it may likewise structure friendship ties [9].",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "In the three reservation schools studied, friendship networks were organized mainly by mutual nominations, friends-of-friends connections, and shared demographics rather than substance use. What varied across schools also matters: prevention programs should be grounded in local friendship patterns rather than assume that substance use organizes peer ties.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These findings reveal a distinct\nrelational ecology within AI-majority reservation schools, where peer ties are organized around structural and demographic commonalities rather than risky behaviors.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "This heterogeneity carries practical implications: network-based interventions for substance use prevention may need to be calibrated to\nschool-specific relational ecologies rather than applied uniformly across reservation settings.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors conducted a cross-sectional survey of ninth- and tenth-graders in three purposively selected schools on one Northern Plains reservation: two tribal schools in different village settings and one private college-preparatory school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In our study, we established partnerships with three schools on a large Northern Plains reservation to conduct a comprehensive cross-sectional social network survey among 9th- and 10th-grade students.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The schools were purposefully\nselected to represent a broad spectrum of social environments on the reservation.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Our selection included a tribal school\nin one of the most geographically isolated and smallest villages (school A), another larger tribal school in a more populous\nvillage (school B), and a private, college preparatory school situated near the largest village in the area (school C).",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey drew 263 respondents, a 77% overall response rate. The network analyses used 247 students with complete network data: 49 from School A, 128 from School B, and 70 from School C.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\nhad an overall response rate of 77% across the three schools. Specifically, 72% of eligible students completed surveys in\nSchool A (n = 52 out of 72), 76% in School B (n = 140 out of 184), and 84% in School C (n = 71 out of 84), for a total sample\nsize of 263.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the 247 participants with complete network data, item non-response was limited to the substance use variables.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "School A (n = 49) was the smallest, located in\na geographically isolated village; School B (n = 128) was the largest, serving a more populous community; and School C\n(n = 70) was a private college-preparatory institution.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Students completed a two-part tablet survey during school hours, reporting their own characteristics and nominating friends through Network Canvas. Community and youth advisory boards reviewed the measures for cultural appropriateness and relevance.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey consisted of two primary sections: the first collected demographic information, measures of AI identity, depression, and substance use; the second collected directed friendship nominations.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "We used Network Canvas, a software\nplatform designed for collecting social network data [32]. All measures were reviewed for cultural appropriateness and\nrelevance by a Community Advisory Board and a Youth Advisory Board from the participating communities prior to finalization [33].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Trained research team members administered the survey during school hours. Students provided responses directly on\ntablets.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcome was a directed friendship network for each school. Students could nominate up to ten close friends in grades 7–12, but the analyzed networks retained only ties among responding ninth- and tenth-graders.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The dependent variable was the directed friendship network within each school.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Students were\nasked to “select up to 10 friends you are closest with in grades 7th-12th at your school.” School rosters within the Network\nCanvas software assisted identification. For analysis, we focused on ties among responding 9th- and 10th-grade students,\nstored in a square adjacency matrix for each school.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Alcohol, tobacco, and marijuana use were measured as separate yes/no indicators of ever having used each substance. The cigarette question excluded ceremonial tobacco use.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants’ use of alcohol, tobacco, and marijuana was\nmeasured through binary variables (yes = 1; no = 0), derived from self-reported data in the survey’s initial section.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Have you ever had a drink (more than a sip) of alcohol, such as beer,\nwine, or hard liquor like vodka?”, “Have you ever smoked a cigarette? (Do not include ceremonial use of tobacco.)”, and\n“Have you ever used marijuana (also called weed, pot, chronic, or nug, including edibles)?”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For refused substance-use items, the authors imputed use if more than half of the peers who nominated that student reported perceiving use at least a few times in the past year. Otherwise, the student was coded as a nonuser. Refusals numbered 16 for alcohol, 14 for tobacco, and 21 for marijuana.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, 16 participants (6.5%) selected ‘Refuse to answer’ for alcohol use, 14 (5.7%) for tobacco use, and 21 (8.5%)\nfor marijuana use. For these cases, we utilized available peer perception data to infer behavior.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "If over half of a respondent’s incoming friendship nominations (i.e., peers who had nominated them as a friend)\nindicated peer perception of them engaging in a specific substance use behavior (response ≥ 2, corresponding to ‘a few\ntimes a year’ or more), the respondent was classified as a user (1) for that variable; otherwise, they were classified as a\nnon-user (0).",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Depression was measured with six CES-D symptom questions, with frequency responses scored from 0 to 3.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey also included a measure of depression, utilizing the Center for Epidemiologic\nStudies Depression Scale (CES-D) [35–38]. This section of the survey consisted of six questions, each probing different\nfacets of depression, such as persistent sadness, feelings of failure, loneliness, and the occurrence of crying spells.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Respondents were asked to indicate the frequency of these symptoms using a scale ranging from 0 (rarely) to 3 (most or\nall of the time).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "AI identity affirmation was the average of six items adapted from the Multigroup Ethnic Identity Measure. Advisory boards helped tailor the wording to the Tribe, and the adapted scale had an internal consistency of α = 0.722.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measurement of AI identity was assessed using six items adapted from the Affirmation and Belonging\nsubscale of the Multigroup Ethnic Identity Measure (MEIM) [39].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "For cultural relevance and specificity within this tribal community,\nitem wording was adapted in partnership with the study’s Community and Youth Advisory Boards, with generic references\nto “TRIBE” replaced by the specific tribal identifier.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "An\naverage score across these six items was computed to represent overall AI identity affirmation. The adapted scale demonstrated good internal consistency in the present sample (Cronbach’s α = 0.722).",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Grade came from school rosters; survey responses were grouped as male, female, or other for gender and straight or non-straight for sexual orientation. Demographic homophily served as a benchmark, while demographic sender and receiver terms adjusted the focal estimates for sorting.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The information about each respondent’s grade level was obtained based on school rosters\nprovided by the participating schools.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "we processed gender information by male (if male is one of the selected options), female\n(if female is one of the selected options), and other (if male and female are not in the selected options).",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Given\nthe low prevalence of responses outside of the straight category, and to simplify the model, we categorized sexual\norientation as straight and non-straight.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "the homophily terms capture substantively meaningful patterns of friendship formation along demographic lines, which we report and interpret as\nreplication benchmarks.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Methodologically, the demographic sender and receiver terms additionally",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "serve as adjustment variables that account for demographic sorting in friendship nominations, so that the focal attribute\neffects can be interpreted net of these compositional forces.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors fitted four exponential random graph models per school: a base model with structural and individual predictors, then three models adding sender, receiver, and homophily terms for one substance at a time. They used Markov chain Monte Carlo maximum likelihood in R.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We employed Exponential Random Graph Models (ERGMs) to model the probability of friendship tie formation within\neach school, accounting for the inherent interdependence of network observations [9,24].",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "For each school, we estimated a series of ERGMs. This included a base model containing structural terms (reciprocity, transitivity [GWESP], degree distributions [GWIDeg, GWODeg], and a two-path term) and core substantive variables\n(demographics, depression, AI identity).",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Subsequently, we estimated separate models for each substance (alcohol,\ntobacco, marijuana) that added sender (nodeofactor), receiver (nodeifactor), and homophily (nodematch) effects for the\nspecific substance use variable.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Parameters were estimated using Markov chain\nMonte Carlo (MCMC) maximum likelihood estimation via the ergm package in R [42].",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The structural specification included mutual ties, geometrically weighted shared partners and degrees, and a separate two-path term to distinguish open chains from closure. The decay parameter for the geometrically weighted terms was fixed at log(2).",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We included standard parameters to account for structural network dependence: reciprocity\n(mutual ties), transitivity (modeled via the geometrically weighted edgewise shared partner, GWESP, term), and the\ndistribution of incoming and outgoing ties (modeled via geometrically weighted indegree, GWIDeg, and outdegree,\nGWODeg, terms) [23,24].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Following Snijders et al. [28], we also included a two‑path term to separate the tendency for\nopen two‑step sequences from triadic closure, thereby allowing the GWESP coefficient to more cleanly capture transitivity.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The decay parameter for all geometrically weighted terms was fixed at log(2) = 0.693, a common choice to aid model\nconvergence and interpretation.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors calculated average marginal effects to express estimates as changes in predicted tie probability and compare their magnitudes across schools.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "AMEs represent\nthe average change in the predicted probability of a tie formation associated with a one-unit change in a predictor variable, holding all other variables constant. This provides an interpretable, population-averaged effect size that is directly\ncomparable across the three school networks [43].",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Pairwise correlations among the focal predictors were modest. The 12 models converged without detected degeneracy, and observed network statistics fell within the central range of simulations from the fitted models.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All correlations were modest (|r| < 0.51), indicating that multicollinearity is unlikely\nto bias the ERGM estimates.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "all 12 models converged successfully within 25 iterations.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Model degeneracy was assessed by inspecting coefficient estimates for extreme values and MCMC samples\nfor non-finite statistics; no degeneracy was detected in any specification.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Goodness-of-fit was assessed by comparing the observed network to 100 networks simulated from each fitted model\n(Figs S1–S12 in S2 File): on degree distributions (indegree and outdegree), edgewise and dyadwise shared partner distributions, geodesic distances, and the triad census.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "In all 12 models, the observed network statistics fell within the central\nrange of simulated distributions, indicating adequate model fit.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked robustness by excluding imputed substance-use responses and by fitting models that omitted the nonsubstance individual covariates.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "sensitivity\nanalyses that exclude these cases entirely leave the direction of every focal effect unchanged and reverse no substantive conclusion",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "To assess whether the absence of substance‑use effects might be due to overcontrol (i.e., the inclusion of depression, AI identity, and demographic covariates), we estimated separate ERGMs containing only structural terms and the\nsubstance use variables (alcohol, tobacco, marijuana).",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Marijuana was the most commonly reported substance in each school. The observed friendship networks had low-to-moderate density, high reciprocity, and moderate clustering.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Substance use prevalence varied modestly across schools: marijuana use was most common (37–43%), followed by alcohol (14–24%) and tobacco (16–23%).",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Network densities ranged from 0.026 (School\nB) to 0.083 (School C), reflecting the low-to-moderate tie density typical of friendship nomination networks where students select from a restricted roster.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Reciprocity rates were high across all schools (93.3–97.7%), indicating that mutual\nfriendship nominations were the norm.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Transitivity\nlevels ranged from 31.0% (School B) to 36.5% (School A), suggesting moderate clustering.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Mutual nominations and triadic closure were consistently favored, while open two-step paths were negatively associated with ties. A subset of students sent disproportionately many nominations in every school, but nominations received were significantly concentrated only in the private preparatory school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Reciprocity was positive and highly significant in every model, indicating a robust tendency for mutual friendship nominations.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Similarly, the positive coefficients for closed triads (transitivity) and negative coefficients for two‑path confirmed that triadic\nclosure—the process by which friends of friends become friends—is a fundamental organizing principle within these AI\nadolescent networks.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The negative and significant coefficients for geometrically\nweighted outdegree (GWODeg) across all schools indicate a centralized structure in outgoing ties, meaning a subset\nof adolescents initiated a disproportionately high number of friendship nominations. For incoming ties (indegree), a\nsignificant negative coefficient was observed only in School C, indicating a centralized popularity structure in that setting.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Students consistently nominated peers of the same gender and grade more often. Shared sexual orientation also favored friendship ties, although the estimates differed across schools and orientation categories.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Strong and significant homophily was present for gender and grade across all schools, indicating that shared gender identity and grade level are primary axes of\nfriendship formation. Sexual orientation homophily was also significant and positive.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual Orientation – Straight\n\n0.017 (0.007)**\n\n0.003 (0.001)*\n\n0.006 (0.004)†\n\nSexual Orientation – Other\n\n0.028 (0.007)***\n\n0.002 (0.002)\n\n0.024 (0.005)***",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Substance use did not consistently predict who sent or received nominations or who chose similar friends. Alcohol users sent more nominations in School C; alcohol homophily and weaker evidence of marijuana homophily appeared in School B. Tobacco homophily was absent, and models with fewer covariates did not change the overall conclusion.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The only exception was a positive sender\neffect for alcohol use in School C (AME = 0.015, p < 0.05), suggesting a tendency for alcohol‑using adolescents to initiate\nmore friendship nominations in that setting.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Alcohol use showed significant homophily in School B (AME = 0.0039, p < 0.01) but not in the other schools or for other\nsubstances. Marijuana use exhibited homophily in School B (AME = 0.0020, p < 0.10), while tobacco use showed no\nhomophily in any school.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "substance use showed no robust or consistent association with sender, receiver, or\nhomophily effects across schools",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Depressive symptoms had no meaningful association with sending or receiving nominations or with choosing peers who had similar symptom levels. A detectable sender estimate in School B and small homophily estimates in Schools B and C were negligible in size.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Depression sender and receiver effects were substantively negligible across all schools; although the sender effect in\nSchool B reached statistical significance across model specifications owing to very small standard errors, its magnitude\n(AME < 0.001) indicates no meaningful association",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "depression homophily was negligible in magnitude (statistically detectable only in Schools B and C;\nAME ≤ 0.001).",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "AI identity affirmation did not consistently predict sending or receiving nominations, and students did not significantly sort by similarity in identity affirmation. School C had a borderline positive estimate for receiving nominations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "AI identity was largely unassociated with sending or receiving ties,\nwith only a borderline positive receiver effect in School C.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "AI identity homophily was consistently non-significant across all schools.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study describes three schools on one reservation; it does not establish that the same patterns hold across other reservations or for AI students at racially heterogeneous schools.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we recognize that the results presented here represent\nonly three schools on one reservation. The history, policies, geography, and culture of this reservation all intersect with\nnetwork formation and maintenance patterns. Our findings may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools,\nwhere Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17] observed different network characteristics.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The comparisons with non-AI research are indirect. The authors could not obtain a contemporary comparison sample of non-AI schools with similar size and rural location.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While a comparison sample of non‑AI schools\nwould strengthen generalizability, identifying a suitable dataset with comparable schools (similar size, rural location, and\ncontemporary data) was not feasible.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the unique cultural and contextual characteristics of reservation‑based\nAI‑majority schools are not readily matched in existing national datasets.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey captures one period of adolescence at one point in time. It does not show how relationships develop at other ages or how networks and behavior change through school transitions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "these data reflect only one\ndevelopmental epoch of adolescents at one time point.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "a single, simultaneous observation\nof the network and of individual attributes cannot capture changes in network formation and behavior over time.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional models cannot distinguish choosing similar friends from becoming more similar through peer influence, and they do not identify causal effects in either direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "cross-sectional ERGMs cannot analytically distinguish selection processes—adolescents choosing friends\nwith similar attributes—from influence processes—friends becoming more similar to one another over time [4,18,48].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "not as evidence that substance use, depression, or AI identity causally affect friendship formation, nor as\nevidence that friendship networks causally shape these attributes.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The measured school friendship networks largely exclude kinship and family relationships. A null result for identity affirmation in school friendships does not establish that identity is irrelevant to those other ties.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Third, kinship networks and familial relationships—which operate largely outside the school-based friendship system we\nmeasured—may be the primary arena where AI identity shapes social ties [27].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Peer-based imputation may reflect false consensus or exaggerate similarity among friends. Excluding imputed cases did not change the substantive conclusions, but a few estimates, mostly in School B, crossed conventional significance thresholds.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "adolescents’ perceptions of friends’ substance use can be systematically biased, including in the direction of false consensus, in which adolescents overestimate\nfriends’ engagement in behaviors they themselves perform—and perceived friend use may diverge from friends’ own\nself-reports [34].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "because the peers providing the perceptions are themselves embedded in the friendship network, imputed values could in principle introduce or amplify apparent behavioral similarity among connected adolescents,\nif peer perceptions are shaped by reporters’ own behavior or by friendship-group norms.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "a small number of coefficients—concentrated in School B, where the excluded cases are most\nnumerous—cross conventional significance thresholds, consistent with the reduced sample size",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Demographic coefficients are descriptive benchmarks, not independent causal effects: the adjustment set was chosen for the focal predictors rather than for causal interpretation of every coefficient.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we caution against interpreting the demographic coefficients\nas independent causal effects of demographics on tie formation: the adjustment set was chosen to support the interpretation of the focal predictors, and a single adjustment set is not generally appropriate for estimating every coefficient in the\nmodel.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Tribe owns the data, which are not public, and the research agreement requires that the Tribe remain unnamed in publications.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Data are not\npublicly available per our agreement with the\nTribe involved in this research, who owns the\ndata and has imposed the restriction.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Our agreement with the\nTribe and its Review Board also requires\nthat we not name the Tribe in publications.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend adolescent social network analysis to AI-majority reservation schools. They use established findings on network structure and individual attributes as benchmarks, asking which patterns travel to this setting rather than treating studies of mostly non-AI students as universal.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We examine each attribute systematically, first summarizing evidence from non-AI adolescent populations and then articulating how the cultural context of AI-majority reservation\nschools may alter these patterns.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "These benchmarks enable a structured\ninterpretation of whether observed patterns in AI-majority reservation schools align with or diverge from established\ntrends.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They combine standard network mechanisms with an account of how cultural norms and school institutions may shape those mechanisms. They retain the idea of broadly recurring structural processes but revise the assumption that those processes operate with the same strength in every setting.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In AI reservation schools, cultural values\nemphasizing kinship, collectivism, and community interdependence provide a distinct social context that likely shapes\nthese endogenous network processes, fostering a cohesive relational fabric among adolescents [26].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Taken together, these findings suggest that while structural endogenous\nprocesses are universal, their specific parameters are calibrated by institutional context.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They challenge the premise in existing network-based prevention that risky behavior is a primary basis for adolescent relationships, and propose investigating strengths-based connections as an alternative foundation for prevention.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The prevalent approach to adolescent social network interventions for substance use prevention often hinges on the presumption that friendship ties are, to some extent, founded on substance use behaviors. Given\nthat this assumption does not align with our findings, it becomes imperative to investigate culturally relevant alternatives\nto understanding of how substance use relates to friendship formation.",
            "page": 16
